-- title:
--   Points of the Taylor–Wiles Hecke ring are classical
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a characteristic-zero discrete valuation ring that is a domain, complete for the adic topology of its maximal ideal and has finite residue field; let $S\subseteq\mathbb N$, let $N\ge 1$ and $r\ge 1$, let $\theta$ be a ring homomorphism from the weight-two level-$N$ Hecke algebra [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) — the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb C}$ of weight-two cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(N)$ generated by the operators $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell\nmid N$, $\ell\notin S$, and $U_q$ for primes $q\mid N$, $q\notin S$ — to the residue field of $\mathcal O$, let $qv:\mathrm{Fin}\,t\to\mathbb N$ have nonzero values, $\alpha:\mathrm{Fin}\,t\to$ the residue field, and put $L=\mathrm{level}\,N\,r\,qv=N\bigl(\prod_i qv_i\bigr)r$. Let $H\le(\mathbb Z/L)^\times$ be a subgroup; `hc` asserts that the operators $\mathrm{op}$ attached to the generators `Gen L S t` commute pairwise, and `hdc` that each such operator commutes with every diamond operator [`CohCarrier.diamondL`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L55) on $H^1(L,H,\mathcal O)$. Let $\mathcal O'$ be a second such ring, an $\mathcal O$-algebra with local structure map, and let $\lambda$ be an $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism to $\mathcal O'$ from `HeckeRing N r qv S 𝒪 H θ α hc hdc`, the $\mathcal O$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathcal O}(ML)$ generated by the operators $T_\ell$ ($\ell$ prime, $\ell\notin S$, $\ell\nmid L$) and $\langle u\rangle$ ($u\in H_r$) acting on the localised cohomology module $ML$. The conclusion produces a characteristic-zero discrete valuation ring $\mathcal O_1$, a domain, complete for its maximal-ideal adic topology, with finite residue field, which is a module-finite $\mathcal O'$-algebra whose structure map is local and injective, and such that one of the following holds. Either (classical case) there exist a Dirichlet character $\varepsilon$ modulo $L$ with values in $\mathbb C$, a weight-two cusp form $h$ on $\Gamma_1(L)$ and a ring homomorphism $\iota$ from the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra of $\mathbb C$ generated by all $q$-expansion coefficients $a_n(h)$ together with all values $\varepsilon(u)$, $u\in(\mathbb Z/L)^\times$, into $\mathcal O_1$, such that: $h$ satisfies [`CuspForm.IsEigenformWith ε h`](def/CuspForm_PrimitiveFormGamma1.html#L19), i.e. $a_1(h)=1$, $a_{pn}(h)+\varepsilon(p)p^{k-1}a_{n/p}(h)=a_p(h)a_n(h)$ for primes $p\nmid L$ (the last term read as $0$ unless $p\mid n$), $a_{\ell n}(h)=a_\ell(h)a_n(h)$ for primes $\ell\mid L$, and $h$ has nebentypus $\varepsilon$ under $\Gamma_0(L)$; $\varepsilon$ is trivial on $H$; $\iota(a_\ell(h))$ equals the image of $\lambda(T_\ell)$ for every prime $\ell\notin S$ with $\ell\nmid L$; $\iota(\varepsilon(u))$ equals the image of $\lambda(\langle u\rangle)$ for every $u$ in $H_r=\ker\bigl((\mathbb Z/L)^\times\to(\mathbb Z/r)^\times\bigr)$; and for each $j$ and each $a\in\mathcal O$ with residue $\alpha_j$, the difference $\iota(a_{qv_j}(h))-a$ lies in the maximal ideal of $\mathcal O_1$. Or (Eisenstein case) there exist Dirichlet characters $\psi_1,\psi_2$ modulo $L$ and a ring homomorphism $\iota$ from the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra generated by their values into $\mathcal O_1$ with $\psi_1\psi_2$ trivial on $H$, $\iota(\psi_1(\ell))+\ell\,\iota(\psi_2(\ell))$ equal to the image of $\lambda(T_\ell)$ for all primes $\ell\notin S$, $\ell\nmid L$, and $\iota(\psi_1(u))\,\iota(\psi_2(u))$ equal to the image of $\lambda(\langle u\rangle)$ for all $u\in H_r$.
--
--   This is the classicality (Eichler–Shimura) statement for the Hecke ring of the localised first cohomology of $\Gamma_H(L)$ at a Taylor–Wiles level: every $\mathcal O$-algebra point of that Hecke ring is, after a finite extension of the coefficient ring, the system of eigenvalues of a weight-two eigenform with nebentypus on $\Gamma_1(L)$, or else is Eisenstein, and in the eigenform case the $q_j$-th coefficient may additionally be required to reduce to the prescribed residual value $\alpha_j$. It is used in the construction of a basis adapted to the inertia action at the Taylor–Wiles primes, where the Frobenius eigenvalue at $q_j$ must be matched with $\alpha_j$.
